Here is a rendering of Chaucer's "Truth" (Balade de Bon Conseyl) in different words while preserving its meaning:

Turn away from the crowd, and abide instead with honesty;
let your own modest possessions be enough, however small they seem.
Hoarding wealth breeds hatred, and ambition brings only instability;
crowds foster jealousy, and prosperity deceives on every side.
Desire nothing beyond what is truly needed;
govern yourself well, you who can counsel others so ably;
and truth will set you free—of this, have no doubt.

Do not exhaust yourself trying to straighten all that is crooked,
placing your faith in Fortune, who spins like a ball.
Great contentment comes through little striving;
so beware of kicking against an awl.
Do not struggle like the pot against the wall.
Master yourself, you who judge the deeds of others;
and truth will set you free—of this, have no doubt.

Accept with humility whatever is given to you;
wrestling against the world only invites a fall.
This place is no true home, but merely wilderness.
Onward, pilgrim! Onward, beast, leave your stall behind!
Recognize your true homeland! Look upward! Give thanks to God for everything!
Keep to the high road, and let your spirit be your guide;
and truth will set you free—of this, have no doubt.

Therefore, you, Vache, abandon your former misery;
cease being enslaved to worldly things.
Beg for mercy from Him who, in His great goodness,
created you out of nothing, and especially
draw close to Him, and pray generally
for yourself and others, for heavenly reward;
and truth will set you free—of this, have no doubt.

